  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...A surface front stalls across north Florida today,\nas a low pressure system moves off of the Northeast US coast.\nAloft, an upper level trough digs southward into Florida, before\nmoving offshore overnight. Locally, this looks to begin a wet \npattern over the next several days. Breezy southwesterly flow,\nwith gusts near 20 mph, will prevent the development of the sea\nbreeze today. Deep moisture (PWATs 2-2.5\") and upper level \nsupport will lead to high coverage of showers and storms area- \nwide today and into tonight. PoPs through this afternoon range \nfrom 40-70%, highest for areas near and north of Melbourne. \nUnfortunately, CAMs have really struggled to nail down convective \nevolution today, with a bit to be desired from run to run \nconsistency on where the heaviest rainfall could occur. The \ndriver for convection will be outflow boundaries and perturbations\nin the upper level flow, unlike our more typical sea breeze \npatterns. Thus, hourly PoPs are quite general overall. Showers and\nstorms will remain possible overnight north of the Treasure \nCoast, with PoPs 30-50%.\n\nAny storms today into tonight will be capable of a quick 1-3\", \nwith isolated higher totals possible in any training bands. Given \nrecent trends, areas along and north of I-4 look to have the \nhighest chance for locally heavy rainfall, but confidence is low. \nAll but southern portions of the Treasure Coast are under a \nMarginal Risk (1/4) for excessive rainfall today. The greatest\npotential for a few strong storms will be across southern portions\nof the area, where lingering drier air in the mid-levels could\nsupport a few wind gusts to near 50 mph. High cloud and\nprecipitation coverage will moderate highs into the upper 80s to\nlower 90s. However, maximum heat indices will linger near 102-106\ndegrees.\n\nFriday-Sunday...Our wet pattern continues into the weekend, as the\nfront remains stalled through Saturday, before beginning to lift\nnorthward on Sunday. Aloft, the upper level trough moves offshore\nthrough the period, though mid-level shortwaves will continue to\ntraverse the area. High coverage of showers and storms each day\n(up to 60-80%), as southwesterly flow persists. However, will see\nthe pressure gradient slacken into this weekend, allowing for the\nsea breeze to develop but remain pinned near the coast and focus\nconvection. Deep moisture will continue to support potential quick\nrainfall totals of 1-3+\", with multiple waves of showers and\nstorms each day. Areas that receive multiple rounds of heavy \nrainfall over several days could see increasing minor flooding \nconcerns. A Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall continues through\nat least Friday, though Saturday could be the wettest day of the \nperiod, with deep PWATs and the pinned east coast sea breeze.\n\nDespite the presence of deep moisture, some drier air in the mid-\nlevels will continue to support a few strong wind gusts up to\n50-55 mph, particularly Friday afternoon. Isolated to scattered\nshowers and storms will remain possible each night during the\novernight hours, especially north of I-4, closest to the stalled\nfront. High temperatures linger in the lower 90s, with peak heat \nindices 102-107, regardless of the high coverage of showers and\nstorms.\n\nMonday-Thursday...The Atlantic ridge axis drifts northward through\nthe period, as high pressure develops aloft over the eastern Gulf.\nLight, generally southerly/southeasterly flow will prevail, \nallowing for a more normal sea breeze pattern to develop. This\npattern, combined with a bit of drier air aloft from the Saharan\nAir Layer will lead to nearer to normal PoPs (40-60%) across the\narea. Lower coverage of showers and storms, with clearer skies,\nwill allow highs temperatures to creep towards the mid-90s over \nthe interior, with lower 90s elsewhere. Overnight lows remain warm\nand muggy in the 70s.",
